# The last matching line wins, so the general rules come first.
#
# eol=lf is deliberate and slightly unusual on Windows. Without it, text=auto stores LF in the
# repository but core.autocrlf=true checks CRLF out into the working tree -- and .editorconfig
# declares end_of_line = lf, so the stated convention would be one the working tree does not
# keep. Pinning it here makes the two agree on every platform.

* text=auto eol=lf
*.cs text diff=csharp
*.csproj text
*.props text
*.targets text
*.md text
*.json text
*.yml text

# The one exemption from eol=lf. Visual Studio and Rider write a solution file with a BOM and
# CRLF and rewrite it that way whenever they touch it, so normalising it to LF would produce a
# spurious diff every time the solution is opened. Keeping it in its native form is the quiet
# option; it is also why this line comes last.
*.sln text eol=crlf
